WEBVTT

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:07.170
Whoa! Wow!

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.940
Who's that?

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.790
That's Koito's second son.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.360
The spoiled rich kid.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.240
<b>Kagoshima</b>

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.120
<b>Koito Otonoshin
Age: Fourteen</b>

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.600
Watch out, idiot!

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.030
Are you trying to get yourself killed?

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.220
Stop right there!

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:45.940
Who the hell do you think you are?

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:50.200
I'm the son of Commander Koito Heiji!

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:54.450
You think you can get away with treating 
a member of the Koito family like that?

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.280
If you want to fight, why not
 use your own name?

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.510
You, old geezer! Give me your staff!

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:05.960
Of course, young master!

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.270
Your swing was straight and beautiful, but...

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.030
What's wrong? Why are you so quiet?

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.240
Are you surprised that 
someone could stop your

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.280
Jigen-style swordsmanship 
with his bare hands?

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:46.140
Or was that the first time a 
grown-up slapped you in the face?

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.130
Both.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:41.720
<b>Golden Kamuy</b>

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:28.390
This vehicle is fascinating. Is it yours?

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:33.420
<b>Episode Forty
Spoiled Rich Kid</b>

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:31.780
My father got it from 
someone he knows in France.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:35.150
And you're riding it without permission?
 Nobody scolds you?

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:36.940
Nobody scolds me.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.320
I see.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:44.200
Oh, where can I find the 
grave of Saigou Takamori?

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.040
You're a tourist?

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.580
Let me apologize to you by taking you there.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:58.990
That's Saigou-don's grave.

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:01.090
Thanks. That saved me a long walk.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:03.780
The view is nice here. 
Let's eat these together.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.370
This is Tsukisappu anpan.

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:10.880
What is Tsukisappu?

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:14.280
The name of the place that makes this anpan.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.440
What's good to eat in Kagoshima?

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.370
Sakurajima daikon radishes, maybe?

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.100
Whose grave is that?

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:37.840
My brother's.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:44.290
He was pale, like mom, so I always 
called him a Sakurajima daikon.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:48.740
But he never got mad. 
He was a very kind brother.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.210
I should've been the one to die.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.150
Tell me about it.

00:04:58.450 --> 00:05:01.370
You should let out what's 
building up inside you.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:03.850
No, I'm sorry.

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:07.160
I've said too much to somebody I just met.

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:09.930
You may think you have a duty to your father

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:14.000
to fill the hole your 
brother left, but I don't.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:25.630
<b>Koito Heinojou</b>

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:25.630
Ensign Koito Heinojou. 
Died Meiji Year 27, September 17th.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.870
The Battle of the Yalu River 
in the Sino-Japanese War, yes?

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:32.280
He was 13 years older than me.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.270
It was when I was 8.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:40.570
He was aboard the Matsushima. 57 people 
died after a Chinese cannon blast.

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:43.880
I heard my father watched the

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:47.910
Matsushima take the hit from 
another ship. He just stared.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:54.730
After he came back, he never scolded me.
 But he also stopped smiling at me.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:06:00.540
I kept imagining what it was he'd seen.

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:03.420
He watched my brother die horribly.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:06.290
Some of the sailors were burned 
to death by the explosions,

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:08.720
others were blown to pieces on the decks.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:12.250
Some of my classmates at 
Kaijou Naval School were talking about it.

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:16.560
Now whenever I'm on a ship for a long time,

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.080
I think about my brother, 
and I get terribly seasick.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:27.720
A man who can only be on a ship for a 
single day can't be a naval officer.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:33.530
My brother might be feeling lonely soon.

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:38.040
We'll be moving to Hakodate 
because of my father's work.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.890
Hakodate?

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:41.650
I think you'll like it.

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:44.690
It looks a little like Sakurajima.

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:48.260
You can look down on the 
port from the mountains...

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:50.880
You know Hakodate?

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:54.070
Tsukisappu comes from Hokkaido.

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:58.020
Thank you.

00:06:58.330 --> 00:06:59.480
We'll meet again.

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:04.310
Will we?

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:09.720
If we by chance meet again,

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:14.660
it means heaven's telling us to 
be friends. Let's do as it says.

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:20.960
<b>Hakodate</b>

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:25.340
<b>Koito Otonoshin
Age: Sixteen</b>

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:25.340
That's Captain Koito's second son.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:28.360
He was a problem child at Kaijou, I heard.

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:31.010
He's taking the exams for the naval 
officer's academy this year, right?

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:34.830
He's probably going to fail, 
isn't he? That spoiled rich kid.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:43.060
You're in the way! Move!

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:52.240
<b>Four
Days
Later</b>

00:07:50.510 --> 00:07:52.240
<b>Hakodate
Koito Residence</b>

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:59.120
And this man the army's sending...
 What's he like?

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:02.890
<b>Mother
Koito Yuki</b>

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:03.210
He's a member of a special organization 
in Tsukisappu, and immensely talented.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:08.090
He's fluent in Russian, and they 
say he's the only man for the job.

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:07.480
<b>Naval Lieutenant
Nakayama</b>

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:12.110
<b>Father
Koito Heiji</b>

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:13.070
And nobody in the Navy speaks Russian, then?

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:20.000
Lieutenant Tsurumi, from the army.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.890
I apologize for coming in via the back door.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:25.480
Let's start by closing the curtains.

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:27.330
We can't plan the operation 
to get back your son

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:29.940
if just anyone can look in and 
see what we're doing.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:38.160
Otonoshin-kun's motorcycle was spotted 
inside the gates of the Russian embassy.

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:40.950
It was left lying there.

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:44.210
The Russian Embassy is 
uninhabited in the summer.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:48.450
We didn't go inside, 
but the place seemed empty.

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:52.460
Of course, Otonoshin-kun wouldn't be there.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:55.430
Are they after money?

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:57.980
If they just wanted the 
child of a rich family,

00:08:57.980 --> 00:08:59.860
there are any number of them in this city.

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:05.300
If Russia is involved in the kidnapping,
 this is a serious matter.

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:12.960
Drink!

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:20.720
I don't speak Russian. Do you speak Japanese?

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:21.600
Quiet!

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.630
<i>I'm the son of a naval officer.</i>

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:33.590
<i>With Russia moving south, 
I knew things have been on edge.</i>

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:36.120
<i>Does this have something to 
do with my father being the</i>

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:37.710
<i>overseer for the Oominato Torpedo Group,</i>

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:42.060
<i>which is near to being completed on 
the opposite shore from Hakodate?</i>

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:48.100
My father would never do what the 
Russian army says for my sake!

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:59.200
<b>Vladivostok</b>

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:59.200
<b>Hakodate</b>

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:59.200
<b>Oominato</b>

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:54.050
When the Vladivostok fleet heads through 
the Tsugaru Strait towards the Pacific,

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:59.200
the Oominato Torpedo Group and Fort Hakodate 
will present a serious threat to them.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:03.640
There are torpedo boats docked 
in Hakodate port right now,

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:05.790
waiting to be deployed to Oominato.

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:11.680
If the kidnapper is Russian, we can assume 
their goal is to destroy these boats,

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:13.800
temporarily neutralizing the threat.

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:20.090
But shouldn't we have received some 
word from the kidnappers, then?

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:25.850
Let's try taking action on our side. 
We may cause something to happen.

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:48.210
It's coming from within the embassy.

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:51.000
Wait, please.

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:53.290
It may be from the kidnappers.

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:56.260
Yes, it likely is.

00:10:56.260 --> 00:10:58.720
But the timing's too perfect.

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:01.800
The Russian Embassy is being watched.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:04.680
Let's pretend we didn't hear it and leave.

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:08.850
We'll always have to be two steps ahead.

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:12.650
One wrong move, and we're finished.

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:15.900
Now is the time to stand strong, Captain Koito.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:21.990
There are 320 buildings with 
telephones in Hakodate.

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:25.170
If we eliminate those that aren't within 
binocular range of the Russian Embassy,

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:27.870
as well as banks and public facilities,

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:32.370
that still leaves us with over 50 potential 
places for our kidnappers to hide.

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.950
We can't barge into each one of them in turn.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:38.340
Any major actions like that 
will show them we're onto them.

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:42.840
When we're ready, we'll head 
to the Russian Embassy again.

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:47.350
And when the call comes in, we'll have them 
put on Otonoshin-kun to make sure he's okay.

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:52.190
Then we hang up, and get the number from 
the telephone operator to help find them.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:54.110
Otonoshin...

00:11:55.250 --> 00:11:57.070
We'll do everything we can.

00:11:57.070 --> 00:12:00.240
Please, do not give up hope.

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:08.080
<b>Golden Kamuy</b>

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:15.970
The phone's here.

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:19.210
But there's a phone at 
Captain Koito's place, too.

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:23.590
The Russian embassy is on 
a hill near Mt. Hakodate.

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:26.510
It's easily visible from much of the town.

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:29.870
The telephone exchange 
shuts down at 1900 hours.

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:33.020
There'll be no calls until 
0600 hours the next morning.

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:36.900
Let's turn on the lights to 
let them know we're here.

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:47.160
If the kidnappers demand I neutralize the 
Oominato Torpedo Group or Fort Hakodate,

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:53.040
and I do it, the Russian fleet 
will attack immediately.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:55.540
A war will begin.

00:12:55.890 --> 00:13:02.630
Hundreds of thousands, no, millions, 
of Japanese will die for my son.

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:06.680
Otonoshin will have to die.

00:13:10.910 --> 00:13:11.660
Eat.

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:20.110
That's...

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:22.420
Where did you find these?

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:25.150
Don't eat them!

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:26.670
They're old.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.010
It's almost 0600 hours.

00:13:30.010 --> 00:13:31.740
The exchange will open.

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:37.080
I have men on standby to start a 
raid the second we find the number.

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:43.400
There's a chance they'll move rather 
than let us find them from the number.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:45.800
Speed is of the essence in this operation.

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:47.090
Lieutenant Tsurumi,

00:13:48.010 --> 00:13:52.100
if the kidnappers really 
think I'd betray the navy

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:55.950
and destroy my own destroyers 
for the sake of my son,

00:13:55.950 --> 00:13:57.850
they must think I'm a fool.

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:01.970
Otonoshin's a child, 
so he may not understand.

00:14:01.970 --> 00:14:04.070
He may hate me, and that's fine.

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:09.070
I'll tell him myself that 
he must die for his country.

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:14.660
You are a true samurai, sir.

00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:25.090
<b>Hakodate Telephone Bureau</b>

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.280
<i>Hello? Is this #18?</i>

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:25.090
Yes.

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:27.380
<i>Putting you through now.</i>

00:14:28.250 --> 00:14:30.860
<i>The fort... The destroyers...</i>

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:31.990
<i>Destroy them!</i>

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:36.740
He's telling us to destroy 
the fort and the destroyers.

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:40.230
As we thought...

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:42.500
<i>Put the child on the phone!</i>

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:52.020
<i>Otonoshin?</i>

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:54.280
<i>Father!</i>

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:57.670
<i>Otonoshin, I'm not going to save you.</i>

00:14:58.030 --> 00:14:59.120
<i>For your country, you must...</i>

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:02.630
I'm sorry I couldn't be a 
son like my older brother!

00:15:07.330 --> 00:15:09.510
Please, pretend I was never born!

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:11.410
<i>Please, pretend I was never born!</i>

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:18.430
Exchange, what's the number!

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:20.060
Ah... Yes!

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:22.060
It's #144!

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:26.020
Then don't connect anyone 
to #144, starting now!

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:27.750
Number 144?

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:28.780
Where's that?

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:31.610
That wasn't on the list we drew up!

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:34.740
Number 144 is the number for 
an army training facility

00:15:35.020 --> 00:15:37.660
that ceased being used several months ago!

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:45.420
So it's an army training facility 
in Hakodate. Does that mean...?

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:46.670
Yes.

00:15:46.990 --> 00:15:48.920
Goryoukaku!

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:51.570
That's six kilometers away!

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:53.130
That's far!

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:55.630
Send the signal to your men 
so they can go there, now!

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:59.230
My men don't know that #144 is Goryoukaku!

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:02.350
It's faster to go ourselves!

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:06.730
Okay, let's go!

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:13.700
The horses are scared of the 
steep hill and won't run!

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:16.620
And there's no time!

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:18.700
Otonoshin!

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:22.140
Captain Koito!

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:45.430
Someone's following us!

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:48.440
He's the one who was watching the 
Russian Embassy from up close!

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:52.890
He can't call the Goryoukaku, 
so he's on his way there.

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:55.820
We can't let him get 
there ahead of us! Hurry!

00:17:19.590 --> 00:17:20.760
If you'll excuse me...

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:27.100
Got him!

00:17:29.530 --> 00:17:30.730
Watch out!

00:17:40.570 --> 00:17:43.310
Otonoshin!

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:54.920
We've arrived at Goryoukaku!

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:02.410
Stay put.

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:06.120
I'll go around back and head in.

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:08.070
Draw their attention from the front.

00:18:09.030 --> 00:18:12.630
Kill me! If I die in battle,

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:15.610
maybe my father might respect me!

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:22.530
Otonoshin!

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:27.210
Is that you, Father?

00:18:30.590 --> 00:18:31.690
Spoiled rich kid.

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:35.300
Otonoshin—

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:53.270
Father!

00:18:56.590 --> 00:18:58.120
Father!

00:19:06.820 --> 00:19:08.040
You're...

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:10.850
So we did meet again.

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:21.890
You're awake?

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:24.360
So you made it, Otonoshin?

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:26.510
You fought well.

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:28.290
I'm proud.

00:19:35.970 --> 00:19:39.490
The one I shot on the way here,
 and these two...

00:19:39.730 --> 00:19:41.850
I'm sure even if we find out who they are,

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:45.490
we won't be able to connect 
them to the Russian government.

00:19:48.670 --> 00:19:53.500
The stuff they fed me was Tsukisappu anpan.

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:56.500
I remembered how I had some in Kagoshima.

00:19:56.900 --> 00:20:02.760
Yes, only the 25th Infantry 
Regiment has the stuff.

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:07.820
This used to be an army post. 
Someone must have forgotten it.

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:10.100
Did it make you sick?

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:15.950
I can't believe the guy who fed me 
that Tsukisappu anpan came to save me!

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:18.150
It's fate!

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:22.320
So Tsukisappu anpan brought 
us together, then?

00:20:24.830 --> 00:20:25.890
Indeed!

00:20:33.850 --> 00:20:36.330
<b>Asahikawa 7th Division</b>

00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:42.060
He suddenly insisted on going 
to the army officer's school

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:45.760
instead of joining the navy.
 And he passed, too.

00:20:46.120 --> 00:20:48.930
As long as he becomes an 
officer I can be proud of,

00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:51.450
I don't care if he does it on land or at sea.

00:20:51.450 --> 00:20:54.780
Take care of Otonoshin for me.

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:58.580
Your son will someday be a key person in

00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:02.100
building trust and overcoming 
the bad relations

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:05.110
between the navy and army.

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:09.180
I promise to exert myself to the utmost,

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:13.410
and do everything I can to learn 
from your tutelage, Lieutenant. Tsurumi!

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:15.460
Are you nervous, perhaps?

00:21:18.650 --> 00:21:24.700
Oh, we found out who those 
three kidnappers were.

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:27.520
All three were Russian sailors,

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:33.750
criminals who were arrested for 
murder and robbery in Otaru,

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:36.440
only to disappear from prison.

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:39.940
So it was as I thought, then.

00:21:43.790 --> 00:21:45.950
He was so cool.

00:21:46.180 --> 00:21:47.350
Indeed.

00:21:56.250 --> 00:21:57.750
<i>Barchonok.</i>

